In article <32261523.3065@ssc.siemens.com>,
aaron smith  <aaron.smith@ssc.siemens.com> wrote:
> To all wizards, gurus, experts, and the like,
> 
> I am currently running DEC UNIX 3.2c [OSF/1] with enhanced security and 
> must add over 1000 users to the system. This is a monumental task 
> regardless of the timeframe which is relatively short. Does anyone know 
> of a script to add users using the adduser command. Just creating the 
> necessary information in the /etc/passwd is not good enough (ie it 
> dosen't work). Also the user accounts must be locked which means that the 
> auth files must be modified in some way hopefully through the same script 
>  or code. It would be greatly appreciated if someone knows of such a 
> beast. Please email your comments.

DEC has a tool called `convuser', which is not supported under 3.2x (but
is part of the operating system under 4.x) which can convert a user from
BASE security to ENHANCED security, and back.

This means that if you can get the users into /etc/passwd you just need to
convert them to ENHANCED security using the convuser tool.  DEC gave us a copy
of convuser for our 3.2 machines last year, and this is the way we've been 
creating accounts on our machines with enhanced security.  It has worked 
reasonably well.

SOAPBOX MODE ON

One of the big things I heard over and over again before the release of 4.0
was that 4.0 was going to have better user management tools.  Now that I've
got 4.0, I would say that the tools are *a little* better, but they're still
inadequate for the job.  Expecting customers to use an interactive tool to
create accounts on a machine is a bad decision, IMHO.  Yes I know about
"expect", but I shouldn't have to write an expect script just so I can
batch-add users to a machine.  There should be one or more *non-interactive*
commands available to create and activate the accounts.  Even IBM's AIX does 
it better (a lot better, in fact) -- there are non-interactive commands to
accomplish every part of account management.

Ditto for purging users.

SOAPBOX MODE OFF

Tim
