> # vdump -0 -CDNu -f /dev/nrmt0h / >>& /fullback.listing
> # vdump -0 -CDNu -f /dev/nrmt0h /usr >>& /fullback.listing

that looks fine to me.

> Then I run vrestore -t to check my dumps and *poof*
> no section two (or three or four).  That one was
> 
> # vrestore -t -s 1 -f /dev/nrmt0h
> 
> which shows alla files on the root.  Very nice.  Then

yes, because you are right at the begining of the tape.

> # vrestore -t -s 2 -f /dev/nrmt0h
> 
> which tells me:
> 
> vrestore: unable to open save-set </dev/rmt0h>; [6] No such device or address

I don't know about the error message itself (expecially because the -s
option isn't really there) but after you have tasted the first dump you
have to issue the following commands:

{assuming that you have started the dump right at the head of the tape}
# mt rewind
# mt fsf 1
# vrestore -t
{in DU the /dev/nrmt0h is optional, since it is the default value}

> 
> how quaint.

yes I know.. I had to restore stuff and I was very puzzled before
learning of this procedure...

