> No -- Linux has the same problem: sizeof(int) == sizeof(long).  You need
> something where these are different.

I've also got to plug a 64-bit-UN*X (in my case Digital UNIX).  We do
a lot of malloc'ing large chunks, pointing fields of structures into these
chunks, and then later dereferencing these pointers.  Alignment becomes
a big issue in this case, and a 64-bit UN*X (at least Digital UNIX)
makes the structs be aligned on an 8 byte bound.  Well, if it's aligned
on 8 byte, it'll also be aligned on 4 byte for the 32-bit platforms.

Another good thing (I think) about Digital UNIX is that it will NOT let
you dereference a NULL pointer (some may argue that this is valid,
though).  Other OS's I've worked on WILL let you do this (AIX, SCO,
UnixWare, Interactive, DG-UX, and I believe HP-UX).  Don't know about
IRIX and Solaris.

Just my 2 pennies -- Tim
