newsgroups: comp.unix.solaris,comp.sys.sun.misc,comp.sys.hp.hpux,comp.unix.internals,comp.unix.programmer,comp.unix.aix,comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.sys.sgi.misc


In article <32189D13.3834@arborsoft.com> Matt Houseman x4408 <mhouseman@arborsoft.com> writes:

: If it were completely up to me, I would choose Solaris 'cause if
: you can get your app to run on Solaris, it'll run on anything!

Not so. (Really, I'm talking from experience.) I do subscribe to your
line of reasoning, but Solaris is not the the way to. No platform that
has sizeof(int) == sizeof(long) resp. sizeof(int) == sizeof(void*)
should be your choice in this case.

In porting your average vintage UN*X app, you'll encounter endless
headaches from missing prototypes, wrong *printf formats, free mixing
of int and long, ...

Before I first came across a 64-bit-UN*X, I'd also recommended
Solaris. These days, I'd recommand Digital UNIX.
