reply-to: lionel@quark.zko.dec.com (Steve Lionel)
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1



In article <4vuq0e$632@hawk.le.ac.uk>, Clive Page <cgp@le.ac.uk> writes:

|>Um, could you explain why LEN returns INTEGER*8?  And isn't that
|>inconsistent with the Fortran Standard, which surely specifies that LEN is
|>to return a "default" integer?  

The Fortran 77 standard just says "integer", since there is only one integer
type.  The Fortran 90 standard does say "default integer".  We chose to
deviate from the standard here because it seemed the right thing to do, with
our support of objects whose size exceeds 32 bits.  It's difficult to write a 
meaningful standard-conforming program that can tell the difference (I haven't 
yet thought of a way to write even a non-meaningful F77 program that could).  
The larger return size shouldn't make an operational difference in an 
application.
