Jianlin Chang wrote:
> That is, which
> should you start with, then porting to other platfroms? There are lots
> of
> difference between stability, and availability of system services to
> aid system
> software programming. For example, HP-UX doesn't allow you to attach
> to a proces
> s from a debugger. Some OS are still not very POSIX compliant. Any
> comments?

The comment about HP-UX not allowing to connect to a process with the
debugger is just blatently wrong.  Try looking up the -P option.

Of course, the best platform is probably the most difficult one.  In
my opinion you should start with a DEC Alpha.  It will catch your
assumptions about pointer and integer sizes and give you an alternate
view of byte orders.

The HP (where I do my development) has a vary "lenient" enviornment.
The compiler will accept constructs that stricter ANSI compliant ones
(notably the IRIX and DEC ones) don't allow.  Also, there are some
quirks in structure vs. pointer passing that cause bugs to go unnoticed
on the HP (by the code actually working) that will bite you on other
platforms.



