We develop for all the above (except IRIX) and may more besides.
Also non-unix platforms.
My initial development is always on Solaris (x86 as it happens,
although that doesn't matter much).

>version of the OS)?

The oldest version which you will support in the field (2.4 in our
case for Solaris x86, 2.3 for sparc), but we use more recent versions
of the development tools, and you also need up-to-date verions of the
OS to ensure you run correctly on them too.

>That is, which
>should you start with, then porting to other platfroms? There are lots
>of 
>difference between stability, and availability of system services to
>aid system
>software programming. For example, HP-UX doesn't allow you to attach
>to a proces
>s from a debugger. Some OS are still not very POSIX compliant. Any
>comments?

Solaris has excellent development tools, which are fully multi-thread
aware for example. I find it much easier to debug and get working an
application under Solaris and then port it to another unix. I also
find this true when going to NT, Win95 or OS/2 - that may be because
I am more familiar with unix; I can't stand OS's which lock solid
on application faults and don't give core dumps...

