In article <01bb8fae$3b022b60$b4020b26@us003614.interramp.com>, "William VonStorch" <wvonstor@interramp.com> writes:
> I currently have an Alpha 2100 running Dec Unix 3.2D. The system has 4
> 2.1GB drives. The drives are inserted in the enclosure in every other drive
> bay. I've tried to add an additional drive to the empty slots and the
> system doesn't recognize them. Also if I move any of the existing drives,
> there not recognized. The question is how do make the other drive bays
> recognize these drives. Is it a firmware setting or hardware switch
> setting? Any help will be appreciated.
> 
> Bill
-- 
The 2100 standardly comes configured with only 1/2 of the StorgeWorks
shelf connected to the internal SCSI (the SW shelf can be configured by
a plug on its backplane to be two SCSI busses, interlaced ABABABAB, or
as a single SCSI bus).  Since the internal SCSI of the 2100 also connects
the CD-ROM drive and any device in the other 5.25" HH internal bay,
you can't really use the other 4 SW slots with the internal SCSI.

What you can do is get another SCSI controller and connect it to the
other 4 slots in your SW shelf.  I'd recommend the KZPAA-AA,
PCI to SCSI-2 (Narrow SE) adapter (about $300) plus internal cables
needed to connect to the SW shelf.  The other possibility is one
of the RAID controllers (available for the PCI or for the EISA bus)
with 3 SCSI busses.  You could connect one of these to the 4 slots
in the SW shelf and then add a 2nd SW shelf (and 2nd power supply)
and connect one or both of the other busses to that shelf.

