followup-to: comp.unix.solaris,comp.sys.sun.misc,comp.sys.hp.hpux,comp.unix.internals,comp.unix.programmer,comp.unix.aix,comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.sys.sgi.misc
organization: SCO
newsgroups: comp.unix.solaris,comp.sys.sun.misc,comp.sys.hp.hpux,comp.unix.internals,comp.unix.programmer,comp.unix.aix,comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.sys.sgi.misc


Jianlin Chang (chang@mjs1) wrote:

: Among the major UNIX OS, Solaris, HP-UX, AIX, Digital-UNIX, IRIX,
: which is the
: best primary platform for UNIX system software development (and which
: version of
: the OS)?

If you intend the final software to run on lots of platforms, the earlier
you build and test on them, the better.

You will generally find that your tools, your libraries and your customers
determine the earliest version of an OS to support.  As an example, you
are unlikely to find vendors supporting Solaris before 2.3.

For the main development OS, I would suggest looking at the development 
hardware (eg you are going to need lots of disk space to store version control 
repositories), and the various tools available (eg purify is an excellent
memory checker, but is only available for a limited number of platforms).

There is no one correct answer to your question, but hopefully this should
have given you some ideas about what to consider.

Roger
