Subject: trimming wtmp (was: /usr filesystem overloaded)
Date: 1996/08/23
Message-ID: <ww4pw4h7j2n.fsf_-_@test-kiosk.eecs.berkeley.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 176067346
sender: robm@test-kiosk.eecs.berkeley.edu
references: <j_erlank.17.000847D6@sames.co.za>
organization: Technical and Computing Services, U.C. Berkeley, Berkeley, CA
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


Someone asked if there was a better way to maintain the wtmp file
instead of just truncating it.  Attached is a script i wrote many
years ago which prunes records from the top of the file, leaving the
most recent records; i have been using it on our Ultrix 4.2 systems
ever since.  Don't know what will happen under DU.  Use at your own
risk.

-rob

#!/bin/csh -f
# usage: trimwtmp [numrec]
# script to trim wtmp
# Rob McNicholas, 1/30/91
# version of 3/18/92
#
# Deletes the first _numrec_ entries from the /usr/adm/wtmp file.
# If _numrec_ is not specified, 1000 is the default.
#
# Suggested use is as a crontab entry, e.g.
# 42 4 15,30 * * sh /usr/etc/trimwtmp 2>&1 | Mail -s "wtmp trim" root
#
# Bugs:
#       Doesn't check to be sure argument is an integer
#

# the default number of records to trim
set numrec_default = 1000
# size of one wtmp record
set recsize = 36
# file to act on
set wtmpfile = /usr/adm/wtmp

# current size of file
set filesize = `wc -c $wtmpfile`
# trim off file name from wc's output
set filesize = $filesize[1]

# number of records to delete
if ( $#argv == 0 ) then
        # user didn't specify how many records, so use default
        set numrec_del = $numrec_default
else if ($#argv == 1) then
        # use number from command line (and hope it's a number!)
        set numrec_del = $1
else
        echo "Usage: $0 [numrec]"
        exit (1)
endif

echo "Trimming $numrec_del records"

# current number of records in the file
@ cur_rec = `expr $filesize / $recsize`
# number of records to keep is cur_rec - numrec_del
@ keep = `expr $cur_rec - $numrec_del`

if ($keep < 0) then
        echo -n "Tried to delete more records ($numrec_del) than actually "
        echo "exist ($cur_rec)"
        exit (1)
else
        # put what we are deleting in a 'old' file
        dd if=$wtmpfile of=/usr/adm/wtmp.old bs=$recsize count=$numrec_del
        # put what we want to keep in a temporary file
        dd if=$wtmpfile of=/usr/adm/wtmp.$$ bs=$recsize skip=$numrec_del
        # trash the original file and replace with our shortened version
        mv -f /usr/adm/wtmp.$$ $wtmpfile
        # compress the 'old' data (-f forces overwrite if file already exists)
        compress -f /usr/adm/wtmp.old
        # say what happened
        echo "Successfully trimmed $numrec_del records from $wtmpfile"
        echo "Old records are in /usr/adm/wtmp.old.Z"
        exit(0)
endif

# EOF: trimwtmp



