In article <4urcqr$5r8@hpg30a.csc.cuhk.edu.hk> rock@cuhk.edu.hk (Rock Chan)
writes:
> I have a problem on my DEC Alpha, it crashed and rebooted ...
> In the OSF error log /usr/sbin/uerf ...
> EVENT CLASS                             ERROR EVENT
>   OS EVENT TYPE                  302.     PANIC
>   SEQUENCE NUMBER                  9.
>   OPERATING SYSTEM                        DEC OSF/1
>   OCCURRED/LOGGED ON                      Sat Aug 10 03:50:25 1996
>   OCCURRED ON SYSTEM                      iii
>   SYSTEM ID                 x00060009     CPU TYPE:  DEC 2100
>   SYSTYPE                   x00000000
>   PROCESSOR COUNT                  2.
>   PROCESSOR WHO LOGGED      x00000001
>   MESSAGE                                 panic (cpu 1): kernel memory fault
> The Digital technician has checked the hardware but couldn't find any problems 
> on the memory or other hardware.

I guess you are running a not-very-recent (pre-2.7b3) version of pidentd.
There is a bug in pidentd, which then tickles a bug in the kernel code
(seek/reads to /dev/kmem from several processes which share the same file
pointer via forks cause a kernel panic on multi-CPU machines). You can
alleviate the problem by including the two lines

vm:
	kernel-stack-guard-pages = 0

in the file /etc/sysconfigtab, or you can use the latest pidentd (which
opens /dev/kmem in each child, not once in the parent).

Hope this helps.

