In article <4usk56$e22@rc1.vub.ac.be>, xchi@ulb.ac.be (Chi Xiaochen) writes:

>> [ No way I'll keep the atributions straight, but summary is that
     someone asked how to find out how much memory is on a system.
     There is no distributed command to find out easily, but the
     getsysinfo(2) system call has a code to get the information.
     Just a small matter of programming... ]


>The physical memory reported by this program is really total physical memory
>installed in a machine, or the memory available to the operating system, that
>is physical memory minus the space occupied by the kernel?

It is really total physical memory.  Early systems stole 2 MB for their
PAL code, but recent systems may manage to give some of that back once
the OS is booted.  It really doesn't matter since the systems support
virtual memory and the size of the kernel is very fluid.  The kernel
memory allocator will wire down paged memory as needed, the buffer
cache can consume most of free memory given the chance and will thrash
against programs if the conditions are just wrong enough and many
drivers are dynamically loadable and will take away memory when
loaded.
>
--
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com




