Drew (flickema@pilot.msu.edu) wrote:

>   I am in need of some suggestions on preparing Ispell for installation.  I
> am new to this platform.  If you have done this, please enlighten me on the
> procedure you followed.


I only had to modify the file local.h and then type make and it
compiled out of the box.  Here is my version of local.h and some
explanation of the changes I had to make:

#define TERMLIB	"-lcurses"
#define BINDIR	"/usr/local/bin"
#define LIBDIR	"/usr/local/lib"
#define ELISPDIR "none"
#define TEXINFODIR "none"
#define MAN1DIR	"/usr/local/man/man1"
#define MAN4DIR	"/usr/local/man/man4"
#define SORTTMP	""
#define MAKE_SORTTMP "-T ${TMPDIR-/usr/tmp}"
#define MASKTYPE_WIDTH	64
#define CFLAGS "-O -Olimit 671"


	The file local.h needs to be modified to point to the 
appropriate directories.  Any other changes should also be made 
here.
	The emacs (ELISPDIR and TEXINFODIR) part caused the make 
to crash.  This can be avoided by setting the relevant directories 
to something non-existent.  You can't set them to a null string 
because the sh test command can't handle null strings.  I guess this
problem arose for us because we don't have emacs installed.  You 
might not have the same trouble.

	I needed to add the cc flag "-Olimit 671" to get fully 
optimized code.

Ian



