: > DU4.0 being binary compatible with earlier versions of Digital
: > Unix. Packages which may cause you problems are Gaussian, Samba, the
: > teTeX binaries, gcc, as well as Explorer.

: In my experience the move is rather smooth. I'm still running many
: binaries compiled under OSF/1 1.2 and 2.0! 

Yes... I think that is partly why I'm frustrated. On the whole, the entire
upgrade process was very smooth, very easy, and there's no way I want to
go back to v3.2. The unfortunate thing is that although most things worked
first up, the things that didn't or indeed don't work just happen to be the
applications and tools most important to our group.

: I have no experience with any of the other tools that you mention, but
: chances are that it is not the "binary" compatibility that is causing
: the troubles.

Whatever. It's even more of a problem when you do a recompile and still
can't get something to function. Or, in the case of Explorer, you're stuck
with the binaries and have to wait until the software supplier is able
to support the new operating system. Gaussian can not be compiled and run
using the latest Fortran, period. I had to recompile with "no shared libraries"
on a system that still had the old compiler installed in order to get it
working, and it took me a week and a half to find that out.

I'm not trying to say people shouldn't upgrade to DU4.0, neither am I 
laying the blame at anyone's feet. Just that if there are other part-time
sysadmins out there like me who wishfully think that there will be no
compatibility problems between 3.2 and 4.0, better they know sooner rather
than later.

