In article <1996Aug4.133535@jeslacs>, psmode@jeslacs.bc.ca (Peter A.
Smode) wrote:

> I have an Alpha running DU 3.2D with a single RZ26 factory partitioned. By
> following the instructions in the online docs, I have successfully converted
> the factory installed UFS partitions (/ and /usr) to ADVFS.
> 
> Reading more of the docs, they suggest that one should have all the file
> systems on a sinlge disk in the same domain in order to improve performance.
> Unfortunately, my procedures have left me with the following /etc/fstab:
> 
>         /dev/rz0b       swap1   ufs sw 0 2
>         root_domain#root / advfs rw 1 1
>         /proc           /proc   procfs rw 0 0
>         usr_domain#usr  /usr advfs rw
> 
> Should I move the root and usr file systems into a single domain (since
> everything is living on the same disk)? Is is possible to do this with some
> mkdir and link "slight-of-hand" or some other mechanism that does not involve
> complete backup/restore? What resources am I burning up with two domains in
> this environment?

The root partition contains very little that is used extensivily during
normal operation.  Mostly it contains files needed to boot the system and
either mount points for other file systems or symbolic links to other file
systems.

In this case I think it would be better to keep things the way they are. 
If you are adding additional disks, you might consider keeping them in the
same domain (which could be part of the usr_domain if you desire.

I would also suggest adding a file set to the usr_domain for "tmp" and
then adding that to your fstab and mounting it on the /tmp mount point. 
This way all your temporary files will not land of the limited space of
the root partition.

