psmode@jeslacs.bc.ca (Peter A. Smode) writes:

> Reading more of the docs, they suggest that one should have all the file
> systems on a sinlge disk in the same domain in order to improve performance.
> Unfortunately, my procedures have left me with the following /etc/fstab:

> 	/dev/rz0b       swap1   ufs sw 0 2
> 	root_domain#root / advfs rw 1 1
> 	/proc           /proc   procfs rw 0 0
> 	usr_domain#usr  /usr advfs rw

As far as I am aware the docs need to make it clearer that you can't
put two file systems in the root domain. This is what the manual page
for mkfdmn says under the -r option:

       -r   Specifies the file domain as the  root  domain.  This
            prevents  multiple volumes in the root domain.  AdvFS
            supports only one volume in the root domain.

I have multiple domains on one disk with no problems. I think I take a
small performance hit from this but otherwise it's fine. /proc isn't a
real domain so don't worry about it. I think you need swap to be on its
own partition but it doesn't need to be on the same disk as your root
domain although that is the default.

the docs are obviously inadequate as this question comes up a lot both
here and on the osf-managers list

clare

