nhurst@iastate.edu (Nathaniel J Hurst) wrote:

>Is there some kind of software/hardware key or protectection that
>will prevent me from installing a used copy of OSF/1 v.3.2 on a DECpc
>AXP 150.  I know that OSF/1 will run on the box, but I am wondering if
>there will be any problem with licensing. Is Dec's "base license" 
>made specifically for each machine, thus preventing me from using a 
>"used" copy of OSF/1.  Or is the base license just a software key that 
>comes with the license and I can install it?

>The actual question is what is the 'base' license?  Is it a license for
>each individual machine or a transferrable key?  It would really help
>knowing before I put down money on a used OS.  Any kind of advice or
>help would be appreciated.

>I would buy the license from DEC, but they charge nearly the price I
>paid for the machine.

>Thanks,
>nate
>-- 
>Nate Hurst         nhurst@iastate.edu	  
 

Here's my understanding of DEC licensing:

Each product can be licensed, and the product may check with the
LMF (License Management Facility) database to see if a license exists
for the product that is being run.

Each license is checked when it is entered with LMF to see if it
passes certain criteria:
- The checksum validates
	The checksum is calculated from the information in the PAK 
	(PAK = Product Authorisation Key) including dates, options, etc
- The number of Units is enough to run the product on this machine
	Bigger machines may need a larger number of Units to run the
	same product, so a PAK for a smaller machine may not work
	on a larger machine
- Options
	The options for different hardware may vary.

Now, for OSF (d UNIX) the OSF base license includes an implicit OSF
USR (user) license for 2 Unix logons.

Of course copying the software without a license is piracy.

Hope this helps.

Mark Perry.





