In article <subskool-3107961400150001@ttye26.southwest.com.au>, subskool@southwest.com.au (User) writes:

>I am attempting to create LSM volumes in order to concatenate two 4GB RAID
>drives. I am using RAID Array 200 (SWXCR) by DEC (re disks).
>
>After creating the volumes with 'volassist make', newfs cannot initialize
>the disks with the newly created dev files. It states that it requires a
>figure for sectors/track. Looking in disktab, the re entry has a -1 entry
>under the "nt" parameters.
>
>It seems that RAID is incompatible with LSM. Does anybody have any
>suggestions as to how I might go about using LSM with SWXCR RAID 5?

Pick a number.  Any number.  You might want to prefer a number that
is close to reality, but most numbers around 100 should work fine.
Alternately, see if LSM will pick a number for you.  Rather than
using "re" don't use anything.  If LSM has constructed a fake geometry
it may be as good as a random number.

Or you could try the underlying disk type get it started.  It will
quickly figure out the volume is larger than a disk of that type,
but it will still use the larger space.

Once you find a number that works you'll want to spend some time
fine tuning the values for how you plan to use a file system.  I've
noticed that when software volumes are created they tend to be
created as "wide" devices that have lots and lots of cylinders
that resemble those of the underlying disk.  For example a stripe
set of four RZ29s may appear to have 14,800+ cylinders (4 * the
RZ29).  A file system created on such a device will LOTS of
cylinder groups, lots of inodes and lots of space tied up in
backup superblock, cylinder group blocks and inode blocks.

Some applications may appreciate the number of files such
a file system can hold.  But other applications may want
to store fewer very large files.  Getting space back on these
requires fine tuning the newfs parameters.

>
