In article <320F2A42.269@silicon.net.my>, "Colin A. Soosay" <cas@silicon.net.my> writes:
|> Do any of you got any ideas as to the maximum numbers of IP addresses that can be 
|> assigned using this method?

The system will allow you to configure an awful lot of them; I'm not
sure if there is any specific upper limit.

On Digital UNIX V4.0 (and V3.2G, and V3.2[CDEF] with the appropriate
patches), you should be able set hundreds or even thousands of aliases
with little measurable performance degradation.  I'm not sure exactly
what is officially supported.

On older versions of Digital UNIX (anything prior to V3.2C, and
V3.2[CDEF] if they haven't been properly patched), you will probably
notice performance problems if you set hundreds of aliases, but
I'm not sure how significant these will be.

-Jeff



