In article <4uj386$rhj@post.gsfc.nasa.gov>, Shawn Hose <shose@pop200.gsfc.nasa.gov> writes:
|> I have a Dec Alpha 2100 using Digital Unix 3.2c and Sybase 10.  At 
|> various sites across the country we have been experiencing serious 
|> network bottlenecks (users trying to access the server).  Is there a way 
|> to configure the server and/or Sybase to lengthen the "keep-alive" time 
|> for various packets. I know how to configure various daemons like ftp to 
|> lengthen the time-out perios, but with Sybase I'm not sure.

On V3.2C, you can change these kernel settings using dbx:

	tcp_keepidle
	tcp_keepintvl

For V3.2C, once you have installed the proper patch (I believe it
is Patch ID OSF350-195), you can change these kernel settings using dbx:

	tcp_keepcnt
	tcp_keepinit

It is recommended that you read one of the standard texts on TCP
(such as "TCP/IP Illustrated Volume 1") before trying to change
any of these values.  Doing it wrong can make things a lot worse.

|> Users are expreriencing lost/dropped connections when trying to work 
|> outside of our regional area (DC).

This could be the result of setting the TCP keepalive timer too short.
However, I believe that the default setting for the TCP keepalive
timer is 2 hours, so if you haven't already changed it, then this
is probably not the problem.  Maybe it's a Sybase problem; if so,
you might want to ask Sybase about it.

-Jeff



